I'm having a little trouble understanding the logic of an argument I have read
several times on this newsgroup recently. The argument is that we shouldn't
introduce any kind of weapon class restriction or proficiency factor, because
that would reduce the number of available playing strategies to one per
character class, thus making the game more predictable (and therefore boring).

Although I have no problem understanding how a game with only a fixed amount
of possible strategies can get boring, I really don't understand how the
introduction of weapon proficiency factors would lead to that (in order to
avoid any possible misunderstanding, let me state again that I have never
proposed to prevent any character class from using a particular type of weapon
'a priori' - the only case in my proposal where that would happen is when the
character in question is too weak to wield the weapon, possibly (but not
necessarily) because of the initial class that he chose). If I choose to play
a barbarian, I get strength and deadly initial weapons, but poor intelligence.
By that choice I make it very unlikely that my character will ever use powerful
spells - not unless he finds a *lot* of potions of gain ability. If I go for
a wizard, I am very unlikely to become as powerful a fighter as a barbarian -
not unless I find gauntlets of power or a good amount of tins of spinach,
plus gauntlets of dexterity or corresponding amount of potions of gain ability.
And even then, I will have less HP's than a barbarian.

If my initial choice hasn't restricted my strategies, then how is weapon
proficiency (which is merely an extension) going to restrict it? Weapon
proficiency means that a wizard will take longer to become good at using an
axe, whereas a barbarian will take longer to become proficient with the
dagger that our wizard could use right away (and which has a higher to hit
probability by the way, whereas the two-handed sword has a lower one than a
normal weapon). A knight hitting a rust trap (or rust monster, acid blob,
whatever) making his long sword basically unusable will be in the same kind
of situation when he picks up this vulgar polearm he found laying around than
a magic-user deciding to upgrade to something more decent like a mace (and I
personally would think twice before giving up a blessed +1 dagger, +2 to hit
and +1 damage, for a +0 mace found in the dungeon!).

If you want fully unrestricted, "no assumptions" strategies, make yourself a
new character class, let's call it the Jolly Dude. He'd start with all
abilities set to 11 or 12, a fair amount of protection (say, studded leather
and +0 small shield), a fairly good weapon (+0 short sword for example), a
fair amount of magic (1 potion + 1 scroll), a fair amount of gold (100gp,
enough for an armour or weapon upgrade at the first shop, or maybe a couple
tools or an extra scroll or potion) and some useful but not exceedingly
powerful intrinsic, like the ability to tell worthless glass from actual
gems. Last, but not least, weapon proficiency would be set to +0 for everything,
without the ability to upgrade to higher ratings. Then you can decide what
strategy you will use without having to worry about the initial choice you
made :-)

